 # ===== Epistaxis Surgical Control =====
 {'topic': 'Epistaxis Surgical Control',
  'concept_id': 'v6-rhinology-allergy-skull-base-epistaxis-surgical-control',
  'stem': 'A 72-year-old man has recurrent right epistaxis 3 days after technically complete endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation. Endoscopy localizes bleeding high on the septum and lateral wall anterior to the middle turbinate attachment. CTA shows no pseudoaneurysm. Because of distorted endoscopic anatomy from prior surgery, the team plans external ligation through a medial canthal incision. Where should the surgeon expect to find the target artery?',
  'choices': ['Along the inferior orbital fissure about 24 mm behind the rim',
              'Within the lacrimal fossa just behind the posterior lacrimal crest',
              'At the frontoethmoidal suture about 12 mm in front of the optic canal opening',
              'At the frontoethmoidal suture about 24 mm behind the anterior lacrimal crest'],
  'answer': 3,
  'explanation': 'High anterior bleeding after successful sphenopalatine ligation points to the anterior ethmoid artery, an internal carotid (ophthalmic) branch not controlled by SPA ligation or external carotid embolization. Through a Lynch-type incision, the periorbita is elevated along the medial wall; the artery exits at the frontoethmoidal suture about 24 mm behind the anterior lacrimal crest, the posterior ethmoid artery about 12 mm further, and the optic canal about 6 mm beyond that (24-12-6 rule). Staying at the suture line protects the cribriform plate above and the optic nerve behind.',
  'why_wrong': ['The inferior orbital fissure lies on the orbital floor and lateral wall and carries no ethmoid artery branches.',
                'The lacrimal fossa holds the lacrimal sac at the front of the medial wall, anterior to the ethmoid foramina.',
                'About 12 mm in front of the optic canal is the posterior ethmoid artery, deeper than the target for this bleeding site.',
                'Correct.'],
  'board_pearl': 'Medial orbital wall 24-12-6: anterior ethmoid artery, posterior ethmoid artery, then optic canal from the anterior lacrimal crest.',
  'curveball': 'The interventional radiologist offers to embolize the anterior ethmoid artery instead. Why is surgical ligation preferred?',
  'curveball_answer': 'The anterior ethmoid artery arises from the ophthalmic artery, so embolizing it requires catheterizing internal carotid territory and risks central retinal artery occlusion with blindness or stroke. External carotid embolization cannot reach it. Direct surgical ligation or endoscopic cautery at the skull base is therefore the standard way to control ethmoid artery bleeding.',
  'learning_stage': 'mastery',
  'focus': 'anatomy'},
